WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.880
Hold your breath. Carbon dioxide accumulates, and oxygen falls.

2
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.140
Yet the urge that defeats you comes mostly from carbon dioxide piling up, though

3
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.180
oxygen contributes.

4
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.680
Why is the controller built that way?

5
00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:21.813
Two questions: where does the rhythm come from, and what sets its rate and depth?

6
00:00:22.473 --> 00:00:26.113
Inspiratory rhythm comes from the medulla, from a small region called the

7
00:00:26.113 --> 00:00:27.393
pre-Botzinger complex.

8
00:00:28.493 --> 00:00:31.913
Smith and colleagues found it in a neonatal rat brainstem in a dish.

9
00:00:32.413 --> 00:00:35.733
That is a reduced preparation, not the adult human circuit.

10
00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:38.533
At rest, the drive is largely chemical.

11
00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:44.793
Carbon dioxide crosses the blood-brain barrier and releases hydrogen ions in brain

12
00:00:44.793 --> 00:00:48.993
fluid. The brain mainly senses those ions, not the molecule itself.

13
00:00:49.753 --> 00:00:53.953
In mice, retrotrapezoid neurons are proton-sensitive and project to the

14
00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:55.113
pre-Botzinger complex.

15
00:00:55.693 --> 00:00:57.733
Which cells do the sensing is still open.

16
00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:01.373
The carotid body sits at the fork of each carotid artery.

17
00:01:01.973 --> 00:01:07.073
It is the main peripheral chemoreceptor for arterial oxygen, carbon dioxide and pH.

18
00:01:07.073 --> 00:01:08.073
It is the main peripheral chemoreceptor for arterial oxygen, carbon dioxide, and pH.

19
00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:14.027
At normal oxygen, human ventilation rises roughly linearly against arterial PCO2.

20
00:01:14.727 --> 00:01:19.187
Against alveolar PO2, the curve is shallow above about 60 mmHg.

21
00:01:19.747 --> 00:01:22.327
It steepens as it enters roughly 50 to 60.

22
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:25.347
Weil and colleagues measured it under isocapnia.

23
00:01:26.147 --> 00:01:30.747
Added inspired carbon dioxide held alveolar PCO2 at its resting value.

24
00:01:30.747 --> 00:01:36.087
Without that clamp, breathing harder lowers PCO2, which restrains ventilation.

25
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.290
Take an adult at rest at sea level.

26
00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.110
Arterial PCO2 is about 40 mmHg.

27
00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.970
Arterial PO2 is about 100.

28
00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:50.990
That is the steep part of the carbon dioxide line, and the flat part of the oxygen

29
00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:55.590
curve. A small PCO2 error gives a large ventilation change.

30
00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.590
A small PO2 error gives almost none.

31
00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.810
Oxygen's leverage lies elsewhere on its axis.

32
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.260
The breath hold ends at the apnea break point.

33
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:10.080
Both gases contribute, and no simple blood gas rule fully explains it.

34
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:15.760
18 volunteers with little freediving experience held repeated maximal breaths, face

35
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:22.080
in cold water. 15 seconds of hyperventilation lowered starting end-tidal PCO2.

36
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:28.660
It fell from 29.0 to 17.4 mmHg, in the 13 with usable gas data.

37
00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:33.340
The mean hold grew from 111 to 133 seconds.

38
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:39.200
The mean saturation nadir fell from 93.6 to 90.6 percent.

39
00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.380
Hyperventilation lowers carbon dioxide and adds little oxygen to the blood.

40
00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.420
In an adult at rest, breathing is a carbon dioxide controller.
